    DNS -.->|"Private endpoint name resolution"| PrivateEndpoints

    Proofprint -->|"Generate PDF"| PrintProvider
    Printing -->|"Idempotent print requests"| PrintProvider
    Notifications -->|"Email delivery"| EmailProvider
    Notifications -->|"Book orderability notification"| PublisherApis

    ACR -.->|"Kubelet image pull using AcrPull"| AKS
    Entra -.->|"Bearer JWT validation"| Frontend
    Entra -.->|"Entra-only database authentication"| SQL
    ServiceAccount -.->|"Federated token exchange"| WorkloadIdentity
    WorkloadIdentity -.->|"Blob, SQL, Event Grid, Service Bus and secret access"| Apps
    GridIdentity -.->|"Managed-identity delivery"| EventGrid
```

- **Solid arrows** show requests, events and data access; **dotted arrows** show identity, image-pull and DNS dependencies. The shared `Apps` connection represents SQL access used by all application containers; the individual blob connections highlight manuscript/proofprint operations.
- Bicep provisions AKS, the VNet/subnets, private endpoints/DNS, ACR, SQL, Blob Storage, Key Vault, Event Grid, Service Bus and managed identities. Containers, external ingress/TLS, Entra app registrations/roles, SQL permissions, secret injection and external provider integrations require separate deployment/configuration. Key Vault credentials are supplied to integrations through approved secret injection.
- Notifications are routed from Event Grid through the frontend to `BookFlow.Notifications`, **not** through a Service Bus subscription. Its SQL receipts provide durable acceptance and retries. Worker subscriptions and Event Grid deliveries have dead-letter handling; the blob container shown is the Event Grid dead-letter destination.
- `BookFlow.LocalProviders` is a ninth container used only for local development and is deliberately absent from the production Azure diagram. The local setup is described in [the application README](../src/README.md).
- This is the **target architecture**, not confirmation of a deployed environment. The [Azure prerequisites](../src/README.md#cloud-prerequisites-that-are-not-deployed-by-this-code) still apply: Service Bus private endpoints require a supported tier (Premium rather than the current Standard), and Event Grid delivery identity/webhook authentication must be configured and validated. Event Grid and ACR currently have public endpoints; their placement inside the resource group does not imply private networking.

### Deploying the Azure resources

The resource-group Bicep template in [`infrastructure/main.bicep`](../infrastructure/main.bicep) creates the private AKS cluster, container registry, private Blob Storage, Entra-only SQL Database, Key Vault, Event Grid topic, and Service Bus topic with filtered subscriptions for each worker. Private endpoints and private DNS zones connect the data services to the cluster. The application containers, API frontend, external email service, and PrintOnDemand integration are deployed or configured separately.

Event Grid sends only background-work event types to the Service Bus topic; each topic subscription applies its own event-type filter. Notification-only events go to the optional HTTPS webhook instead of being enqueued.

Deploy it with Azure CLI after creating a resource group. The SQL administrator must be a Microsoft Entra security group:

```powershell
az deployment group create `
  --resource-group "RESOURCE_GROUP_NAME" `
  --template-file infrastructure/main.bicep `
  --parameters sqlAdministratorObjectId="GROUP_OBJECT_ID" sqlAdministratorName="GROUP_DISPLAY_NAME"
```

`notificationWebhookUrl` is optional. Set it to the secured API frontend's HTTPS notification endpoint to create the Event Grid notification subscription; the endpoint must handle Event Grid's validation handshake. The template outputs the resource names, Event Grid endpoint, and workload identity client ID and service-account subject. Configure the `bookflow` Kubernetes namespace and `bookflow` service account to use that workload identity. Grant the identity its required SQL database roles separately; SQL access is not enabled by a server-level role assignment.

## Running locally

The .NET 10 applications, separate unit-test projects, Dockerfiles, and self-contained Compose environment are available in [`src`](../src/README.md). Start with the build/test and Docker instructions there. The Compose environment uses persistent SQLite/file storage, a durable local event adapter, and authenticated provider test doubles; it does not require Azure credentials. The same applications provide Azure SQL, Blob Storage, Event Grid, and Service Bus adapters for deployment.

To use the Azure emulators instead of the self-contained local adapters, the architecture can also be approximated with Rancher Desktop and the following substitutes. This alternate emulator configuration is not included in Compose. The Bicep template deploys real Azure resources and does not run against emulators.

1. Install Rancher Desktop, select the dockerd (moby) container engine, and wait for it to be ready. Use the Rancher Docker CLI and the `default` context as described in [the application setup](../src/README.md#local-docker-with-rancher-desktop).
2. Run [Azurite](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/storage/common/storage-use-azurite) for manuscript and proofprint blobs. For example:

   ```powershell
   docker run --rm --name bookflow-azurite `
     -p 10000:10000 -p 10001:10001 -p 10002:10002 `
     mcr.microsoft.com/azure-storage/azurite
   ```

   This starts a temporary local storage instance; add a mounted data directory if you need its data to survive container removal.
3. Use the [Azure Service Bus emulator](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/service-bus-messaging/overview-emulator) for queues, topics, and subscriptions. Follow its setup instructions to provide the required SQL Server dependency and entity configuration, including the BookFlow topic and consumer subscriptions. The emulator is for development and testing only; its entities and messages do not persist across container restarts, and it does not support Entra ID or virtual-network integration.
4. Run SQL Server in a local container and create the BookFlow database using the application's migrations. An emulator-specific configuration/adapter is required for local SQL authentication; the Azure mode deliberately requires Entra-only SQL authentication.
5. Configure additional emulator-specific adapters for the Web App, API frontend, and workers. The supplied `Local` mode uses SQLite/file storage rather than Azurite or the bus emulator. When application containers connect to emulators in Compose, use Compose service names as hostnames; `localhost` inside a container refers to that container, not the host.
6. Adapt the local event-dispatch adapter to publish relevant work to the Service Bus emulator and forward notification event types to the API frontend. Event Grid routing, managed identities, private endpoints, AKS scaling, and cloud dead-letter behavior are not emulated by this setup.
7. Point email, PrintOnDemand, and publisher integrations at test doubles or mock endpoints so local runs do not contact external systems.

These alternate dependencies need their own Compose configuration. Local testing is useful for developing and exercising workflow logic, but it does not reproduce Azure networking, identity, delivery guarantees, or production scaling; verify those behaviors in an Azure test environment.

## Reliability and event delivery

Calls to external systems such as the PrintOnDemand API, email service, and publisher APIs use timeouts and bounded retries with exponential backoff and jitter for transient network errors and timeouts. Do not retry permanent client errors. Use idempotency keys when a request may have succeeded before timing out, preventing duplicate print orders or other side effects. Event consumers deduplicate by event ID and tolerate out-of-order delivery.

Azure Event Grid provides at-least-once delivery, not exactly-once delivery. Failed deliveries are retried according to the subscription policy, so consumers may receive duplicate or out-of-order events. Configure maximum delivery attempts and event time-to-live, and enable a dead-letter destination so undelivered events can be investigated or replayed. See [Azure Event Grid delivery and retry](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/event-grid/delivery-and-retry).
